STEP: 1. Verify Initial Location with 'pwd'
EXPLANATION: In production troubleshooting, you must always verify your current working directory to avoid issuing destructive commands in unintended directories.
RUNNING COMMAND: pwd
----------------------------------------------------------------------
/root/sre-foundations/day1-fhs-cli

======================================================================
STEP: 2. Navigate to /etc via Absolute Path
EXPLANATION: We use an absolute path starting with '/' to move into /etc, the repository of static host configurations.
RUNNING COMMAND: cd /etc && pwd
----------------------------------------------------------------------
/etc

======================================================================
STEP: 3. View System Release & Hostname Configs
EXPLANATION: SREs inspect /etc/os-release to determine the exact Linux distribution and kernel compatibility when debugging software issues.
RUNNING COMMAND: cat /etc/os-release | grep -E '^(PRETTY_NAME|VERSION_ID)='
----------------------------------------------------------------------
PRETTY_NAME="Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS"
VERSION_ID="24.04"

======================================================================
STEP: 4. Inspect DNS Nameserver Configuration
EXPLANATION: /etc/resolv.conf defines where the host sends DNS lookup requests. Corrupted nameservers lead directly to network resolution failures.
RUNNING COMMAND: cat /etc/resolv.conf | grep 'nameserver'
----------------------------------------------------------------------
nameserver 8.8.8.8
nameserver 1.1.1.1

======================================================================
STEP: 5. Navigate from /etc to /var/log via Relative Path
EXPLANATION: Using the relative navigation anchor '..' allows you to traverse up to root and down to /var/log without writing an absolute path.
RUNNING COMMAND: cd ../var/log && pwd
----------------------------------------------------------------------
./day1-fhs-navigation.sh: line 21: cd: ../var/log: No such file or directory

