======================================================================
STEP: 9. Inspect CPU Architecture from Kernel Memory
EXPLANATION: /proc/cpuinfo exposes raw CPU metrics directly from kernel memory. It consumes 0 bytes of physical disk space.
RUNNING COMMAND: grep -m 1 'model name' /proc/cpuinfo && echo -n 'Total Logical Cores: ' && grep -c ^processor /proc/cpuinfo
----------------------------------------------------------------------
model name	: Intel Xeon E312xx (Sandy Bridge, IBRS update)
Total Logical Cores: 1

======================================================================
STEP: 10. Inspect RAM State from /proc/meminfo
EXPLANATION: /proc/meminfo gives granular memory telemetry. SREs look at 'MemAvailable', not 'MemFree', to evaluate memory saturation before OOM conditions trigger.
RUNNING COMMAND: grep -E '^(MemTotal|MemFree|MemAvailable|Buffers|Cached):' /proc/meminfo
----------------------------------------------------------------------
MemTotal:        1948912 kB
MemFree:         1173804 kB
MemAvailable:    1516788 kB
Buffers:           42724 kB
Cached:           413300 kB

======================================================================
STEP: 11. Inspect the Shell Process Environment via /proc/self/environ
EXPLANATION: /proc/self is a dynamic symlink to the process currently reading it. Environment variables are stored as null-byte ('\0') separated strings, so we translate them to newlines.
RUNNING COMMAND: cat /proc/self/environ | tr '\0' '\n' | grep -E '^(PATH|USER|HOME|SHELL)='
----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHELL=/bin/bash
HOME=/root
USER=root
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/local/go/bin:/snap/bin

======================================================================
STEP: 12. Inspect Cgroups v2 & Storage Interfaces in /sys
EXPLANATION: /sysfs exposes physical device properties and control group limits used by Docker and Kubernetes to restrict memory and CPU.
RUNNING COMMAND: ls -d /sys/fs/cgroup /sys/block /sys/class/net
----------------------------------------------------------------------
/sys/block
/sys/class/net
/sys/fs/cgroup

======================================================================
STEP: 13. Return Home with 'cd ~' and Verify Final Location
EXPLANATION: Using '~' immediately takes you to your user's home directory regardless of your current position in the filesystem tree.
RUNNING COMMAND: cd ~ && pwd
----------------------------------------------------------------------
/root

